-- title:
--   Finitely many horizontal primes contain a nonzero element
-- statement:
--   Let $W$ be a commutative ring which is a domain and a discrete valuation ring, and which is adically complete with respect to its maximal ideal. Let $\varpi \in W$ be irreducible, let $e$ be a natural number with $1 \le e$, and form the crossing model $R =$ `UVCrossingModel W (ϖ ^ e)`, that is the quotient of the ring $W[[X_0,X_1]]$ of formal power series in two variables indexed by `Fin 2` by the ideal generated by the single element $X_0X_1 - \varpi^{e}$ (the constant power series attached to $\varpi^{e}$). Write `const (ϖ ^ e) ϖ` for the class in $R$ of the constant power series with value $\varpi$. Let $x \in R$ be nonzero. The assertion is that the set of points $P$ of the prime spectrum of $R$ whose underlying prime ideal $P$ satisfies the three conditions: $P \neq \bot$, the class of $\varpi$ does not lie in $P$, and $x$ lies in $P$, is a finite set. In other words, a nonzero element of $R$ lies in only finitely many nonzero primes of $R$ that do not contain the image of $\varpi$.
--
--   The ring $R = W[[U,V]]/(UV - \varpi^{e})$ is the completed local ring of a node of width $e$ on an arithmetic surface over $W$, and the primes occurring here are the nonzero primes not containing $\varpi$, i.e. the horizontal primes of $\operatorname{Spec} R$; the statement says that the divisor of a nonzero function on the crossing model is supported on finitely many horizontal components. It underlies the divisor and order computations on supersingular annuli used in the component-group and specialization arguments that cite it.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_UVCrossingModel_setOf_horizontal_mem_finite.lean

